  <Section id="owner" title="When every feature got the same workflow" level={3}>
    <Fragment slot="note">Source: <a href={issue(189)}>Issue #189</a>, <a href={pull(194)}>PR #194</a>, 27 October 2026.</Fragment>
    <p>Nothing computed how risky a change was, so every feature went through the same steps, whatever it changed. The change computes a risk level from one policy file. Its three review rounds found nothing. The owner sharpened the scope in a comment and, before the merge, caught a defect: a failed listing of the changed files fell back to the default level instead of the highest.</p>
    <details class="proof">
      <summary>Records: figures, the owner's part and the changed files</summary>
      <CaseSummary>
        <Fragment slot="changed">The risk level of a feature is computed from one policy file.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="rounds">3, none with findings.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="found">Nothing. The owner found a defect before the merge: a failed listing of the changed files fell back to the default level.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="result"><a href={pull(194)}>PR #194</a>, merged by the owner with the required approval, 27 October, 14:47 UTC.</Fragment>
      </CaseSummary>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>tokens</dt><dd>about 3.2 million in all, about 730,000 of them for review. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>interventions</dt><dd>1: the owner resumed the implementer by hand. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>active run time</dt><dd>No single figure: the feature took four runs, because the owner resumed the implementer by hand. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>total lead time</dt><dd>2 h 18 min, from the opening of the Issue, 12:29 UTC, to the merge; waiting and active time are not separated here. Source: the timestamps on GitHub.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p class="lede">Nothing in the workflow computed how risky a change was, so every feature went through the same steps, whatever it changed. #189 introduced the risk level, computed from one policy file. The owner sharpened its scope in a comment, and caught a defect before the merge.</p>
      <ul class="rules">
        <li><b>A comment as a decision.</b> After the first run, <a href={comment(189, "6443105170")}>the owner's comment</a> set two rules from experience with another project: an escalation reads only changed or removed lines of existing files, and a feature already in progress is not refused.</li>
        <li><b>A resumed session.</b> The owner resumed the implementer by hand, which the run record notes as an intervention. The record also shows the comment passed on to the resumed implementer and to each later review.</li>
        <li><b>A correction before the merge.</b> The owner found that a failed listing of the changed files fell back to the default level. The fix makes it high, with a test that fails against the earlier commit and passes with the fix, as the completion record in the pull request states.</li>
        <li><b>Three rounds, no findings.</b> Review by <code>codex gpt-6-luna</code>, ten of ten criteria met; full verifications of 556, 575 and 572 seconds.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Source files changed by <a href={pull(194)}>PR #194</a>: {changed[194].map((path, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={repoFile(path)}><code>{path}</code></a></>))}.</p>
    </details>
  </Section>

  <Section id="repair" title="When a missed target sent the agent to repair" level={3}>
    <Fragment slot="note">Source: <a href={issue(193)}>Issue #193</a>, <a href={pull(199)}>PR #199</a>, 27 October 2026.</Fragment>
    <p>A check against a time or size target failed the verification when the target was missed, and the builder was sent to repair it without a diagnosed problem. The change reports such a check as a measurement instead. The review raised the same major finding twice: the first fix still treated an exit status that a measurement can return as a failure. The second fix held.</p>
    <details class="proof">
      <summary>Records: figures, review rounds and the changed files</summary>
      <CaseSummary>
        <Fragment slot="changed">A check against a target for a duration or a size became a measurement that is reported, not repaired.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="rounds">4. Rounds 1 and 2 raised findings, all fixed; rounds 3 and 4 passed.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="found">In round 2, the major finding of round 1 again, as unresolved: the fix treated an exit status that a measurement can return as a failure.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="result"><a href={pull(199)}>PR #199</a>, merged by the owner with the required approval, 27 October, 19:48 UTC.</Fragment>
      </CaseSummary>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>tokens</dt><dd>about 7.2 million in all, about 1.0 million of them for review and fixes. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>interventions</dt><dd>none recorded. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>active run time</dt><dd>1 h 18 min, from the start of the run to the pull request. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>waiting time</dt><dd>About 5 h 59 min, before the run started and until the merge together.</dd>
        <dt>total lead time</dt><dd>7 h 17 min, from the opening of the Issue, 12:31 UTC, to the merge. Source: the timestamps on GitHub.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p class="lede">A check that measures a duration or a size against a target failed the verification when the target was missed, and the scripts sent the implementer to repair it, with no diagnosed problem. #193 made such budget checks measurements that are reported, not repaired.</p>
      <p>In round 1 the reviewer found that a failing timeout utility was reported as an exceeded budget (major) and that a budget's output was cut short (minor); the triage decided to fix both. In round 2 the reviewer raised the major finding again, as “Unresolved M1”: the fix now treated exit status 128 as a failure of the call, though a measurement can return it. A second fix round resolved it, and rounds 3 and 4 passed. Each round's report and triage is <a href={issue(193)}>on the Issue</a>.</p>
      <p>Source files changed by <a href={pull(199)}>PR #199</a>: {changed[199].map((path, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={repoFile(path)}><code>{path}</code></a></>))}.</p>
    </details>
  </Section>

  <Section id="short" title="When one round is enough" level={3}>
    <Fragment slot="note">Source: <a href={pull(205)}>PR #205</a> and <a href={pull(213)}>PR #213</a>, 28 October 2026.</Fragment>
    <p class="lede">Most features do not need several rounds. These two had a clear problem and a clear request, and the first review passed.</p>
    <h4>When the reviewer saw untouched records as deleted</h4>
    <p>The reviewer was shown the workflow's own approval records as deleted, though the change never touched them. The change hides from the reviewer the paths that the content fingerprint leaves out. One implementation session, one review, nothing found. Source: <a href={issue(204)}>Issue #204</a>.</p>
    <details class="proof">
      <summary>Records of #204: figures and the changed files</summary>
      <CaseSummary>
        <Fragment slot="changed">The reviewer is shown no change in the paths that the fingerprint, the hash that identifies the reviewed content, leaves out.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="rounds">1, passed.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="found">Nothing.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="result"><a href={pull(205)}>PR #205</a>, merged by the owner with the required approval, 28 October, 08:09 UTC.</Fragment>
      </CaseSummary>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>tokens</dt><dd>about 290,000 in all, about 90,000 of them for review. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>interventions</dt><dd>none recorded. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>active run time</dt><dd>38 min, from the start of the run to the pull request. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>waiting time</dt><dd>About 1 h 52 min, before the run started and until the merge together.</dd>
        <dt>total lead time</dt><dd>2 h 30 min, from the opening of the Issue, 05:38 UTC, to the merge. Source: the timestamps on GitHub.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p><a href={issue(204)}>#204</a>: the reviewer was shown the workflow's own approval records as deleted, though the change never touched them. It ran from <code>run-feature.sh</code> to the pull request in 38 minutes: one implementation session, one verification of 909 seconds reused twice, one review that passed.</p>
      <p>Source files changed by #204 in <a href={pull(205)}>PR #205</a>: {changed[205].map((path, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={repoFile(path)}><code>{path}</code></a></>))}.</p>
    </details>
    <h4>When an agent could build before the owner decided</h4>
    <p>An agent could start building while a decision that only the owner can make was still open. The change adds the clarification step: the agent stops and asks instead. The request waited six weeks in the backlog; the run itself was short, and its single review passed. Source: <a href={issue(16)}>Issue #16</a>.</p>
    <details class="proof">
      <summary>Records of #16: figures and the changed files</summary>
      <CaseSummary>
        <Fragment slot="changed">The clarification gate: an agent stops and asks before it builds while a decision that only the owner can make is open.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="rounds">1, passed.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="found">Nothing.</Fragment>
        <Fragment slot="result"><a href={pull(213)}>PR #213</a>, merged by the owner with the required approval, 28 October, 16:34 UTC.</Fragment>
      </CaseSummary>
      <dl class="facts">
        <dt>tokens</dt><dd>about 390,000 in all, about 70,000 of them for review. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>interventions</dt><dd>none recorded. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>active run time</dt><dd>26 min, from the start of the run to the pull request. Source: the run record.</dd>
        <dt>waiting time</dt><dd>About 43 days, almost all of it in the backlog before the run.</dd>
        <dt>total lead time</dt><dd>43 days, from the opening of the Issue on 15 September to the merge. Source: the timestamps on GitHub.</dd>
      </dl>
      <p><a href={issue(16)}>#16</a> waited in the backlog for six weeks. The run took 26 minutes: the implementer received the owner's two comments with the Issue, which settled the design, and the single review passed.</p>
      <p>Source files changed by #16 in <a href={pull(213)}>PR #213</a>: {changed[213].map((path, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ", "}<a href={repoFile(path)}><code>{path}</code></a></>))}.</p>
    </details>
  </Section>

