  <Section id="absent" title="What is not shown" level={3}>
    <Fragment slot="note"><a href={repoFile(".agents/policies/risk.conf")}><code>risk.conf</code></a> of this repository; <a href={href("docs/risk-policy/")}>Risk policy</a>; <a href={issue(170)}>Issue #170</a>.</Fragment>
    <ul class="rules">
      <li><b>A low-risk run.</b> A feature is low risk only when the project's risk policy makes every path it changes low, by a <code>low:</code> class, by <code>default: low</code>, or by <code>profile: light</code>, which adds a low class for documentation and process files; and nothing raises the level. This repository has none of these, so none of its features was low, and this page does not invent one. Under the shipped rules a low feature is still reviewed. With <code>low: review: none</code> or <code>profile: light</code> it is committed without a review; the commit and the pull request say so, and the merge then needs your approval.</li>
      <li><b>The records of FightAnalytics.</b> Its repository is private, so this page shows no run, review, figure or pull request of it, and no claim about which of its functions were built with Codeyard.</li>
      <li><b>The run record of Physics by Construction.</b> Its run records stay on the owner's machine, so its case shows no tokens, interventions, verification times or active run time; it shows what the project published on GitHub.</li>
      <li><b>A cost in money.</b> The run records count tokens but no prices, and a price depends on the plan or the account. This page gives no amount.</li>
      <li><b>Onboarding an existing repository.</b> An existing repository can adopt the workflow (<a href={href("docs/adopting/")}>Adopting</a>). A pilot on a private, existing application ran on 6 November 2026; <a href={issue(170)}>its report on Issue #170</a> describes it in general terms, and the fixes it led to were <a href={issue(299)}>#299</a>, <a href={issue(300)}>#300</a> and <a href={issue(306)}>#306</a>. Its records are private, so there is no run of it to show.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section id="sources" title="Where the figures come from" level={3}>
    <p>Each figure on this page has one of three sources:</p>
    <Table>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">source</th><th scope="col">what it holds</th><th scope="col">where</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Issue comments</th><td data-col="what it holds">The review report and the triage of every round with findings, published by <code>triage-review.sh</code>; with the Issue itself, its acceptance criteria, the owner's word and the time it was opened</td><td data-col="where">GitHub, linked above; for Physics by Construction, on <a href={pbcIssue(388)}>its Issue #388</a></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Pull request</th><td data-col="what it holds">Completion record, verification, review line, risk level and merge approval, written by <code>finish-feature.sh</code> and <code>publish-feature.sh</code>; for Physics by Construction also the measured result in its summary; when it was opened and merged, and the result of its checks</td><td data-col="where">GitHub, linked above</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Run record</th><td data-col="what it holds">Times, agents, models and levels, tokens per agent call, verification seconds and reuses, interventions, comments passed on</td><td data-col="where" class="k">.agents/records/feature-&lt;n&gt;.jsonl, on the maintainer's machine</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </Table>
    <p>Times are given in three parts, where the sources have them:</p>
    <dl class="facts">
      <dt>active run time</dt><dd>From the start of <code>run-feature.sh</code> to the pull request, from the run record: the agents' sessions, the checks and the review rounds.</dd>
      <dt>waiting time</dt><dd>The rest of the lead time: before the run started, while the request waited, and after the pull request opened, until the owner merged it.</dd>
      <dt>total lead time</dt><dd>From the opening of the Issue to the merge of its pull request, both timestamps on GitHub. It is the waiting time and the active run time together.</dd>
    </dl>
    <p>The case of Physics by Construction uses only the first two sources: its run record stays on the owner's machine, so the figures that only a run record gives, tokens, verification seconds, interventions and the active run time, are left out for it.</p>
    <p class="needs">The run records are local by design and not published; the figures from them are copied here as recorded, through <code>report-runs.sh</code>. Durations and tokens depend on the machine, the models and the size of the test suite, and are not benchmarks. No figure here is a price: the records hold no prices.</p>
  </Section>
